---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";

const title = "WiFi connected but no internet: 8 fixes that work";
const description = "Connected to WiFi but nothing loads? Check these 8 things in order, from a quick restart to router lights, DNS and a stuck connection.";

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://arverma.dev/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: "https://arverma.dev/guides/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "WiFi connected but no internet", item: "https://arverma.dev/guides/wifi-connected-no-internet/" },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="section guide-page">
    <p class="section-label"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} />Guide</p>
    <h1 class="guide-title">WiFi connected but no internet: 8 fixes that work</h1>
    
    <div class="guide-content">
      <p class="guide-lead">
        Your phone or laptop shows the WiFi icon with full bars. It says connected. But when you try to load a page, 
        nothing happens. This guide walks through the checks I run every time this happens, in order, starting with 
        the quickest fixes.
      </p>

      <h2 class="guide-h2"><Icon name="info" size={16} />What "connected, no internet" means</h2>
      <p>
        When your device says connected but no internet, it means your device can talk to the router, but the router 
        cannot reach the internet. The WiFi link between your device and the router is fine. The problem is somewhere 
        between the router and your ISP.
      </p>

      <h2 class="guide-h2"><Icon name="smartphone" size={16} />Check one device or all</h2>
      <p>
        First, check if the problem is on one device or every device. If only your laptop cannot load pages but your 
        phone works fine, the issue is with the laptop, not the router. Restart that device and check its WiFi settings.
      </p>
      <p>
        If every device shows connected but nothing loads, the router or the ISP link is the problem. Keep reading.
      </p>

      <h2 class="guide-h2"><Icon name="refresh-cw" size={16} />Restart in the right order</h2>
      <p>
        A full restart clears most connection issues. Turn off the router and the modem (if you have a separate modem). 
        Wait 30 seconds. Turn on the modem first, wait until its lights are stable, then turn on the router. 
        Wait 2 minutes for everything to connect.
      </p>
      <p>
        This forces the router to get a fresh connection from the ISP.
      </p>

      <h2 class="guide-h2"><Icon name="activity" size={16} />Router lights (LOS, PON, internet)</h2>
      <p>
        Check the lights on your router. Most routers have a light labeled Internet, WAN, or PON. If this light is off 
        or red, the router is not getting a signal from the ISP. This is usually a fiber cut, a loose cable, or an 
        account issue.
      </p>
      <p>
        On fiber routers (Airtel, Jio), look for the LOS or PON light. LOS stands for Loss of Signal. If the LOS light 
        is on or blinking red, the fiber cable is disconnected or damaged. Check the cable at the back of the router 
        and at the wall outlet. If it looks fine, call your ISP.
      </p>

      <h2 class="guide-h2"><Icon name="globe" size={16} />DNS and Private DNS</h2>
      <p>
        Sometimes the router is fine, but the DNS server it uses is down. DNS translates names like google.com into 
        IP addresses. If DNS is broken, pages will not load even though the internet connection is working.
      </p>
      <p>
        Try changing your DNS to Google DNS (8.8.8.8 and 8.8.4.4) or Cloudflare DNS (1.1.1.1 and 1.0.0.1). 
        On Android, go to Settings > Network & Internet > Private DNS and set it to dns.google or one.one.one.one. 
        On a laptop, change the DNS in your network settings.
      </p>

      <h2 class="guide-h2"><Icon name="zap" size={16} />Stuck idle connection and how a wake-up helps</h2>
      <p>
        Some routers go idle when no one is using the connection. When you try to load a page, the router takes a few 
        seconds to wake up. Sometimes it does not wake up at all, and you get "connected but no internet."
      </p>
      <p>
        I built <a href="/projects/wifi-hammer/">WiFi Hammer</a> to fix this. It is a Chrome extension that wakes the 
        connection with one click by sending real traffic to Fast.com and Netflix servers. Auto-hammer runs a light check 
        every 5 minutes to 6 hours (30 minutes default, configurable) and only hammers after two failed checks. 
        Hammer Hard runs a full speed test and can use a lot of data.
      </p>
      <p>
        WiFi Hammer does not make your internet faster. It does not increase your bandwidth or change your plan. 
        It does not reset the router. It only wakes a stuck connection. If your internet is working but slow, WiFi Hammer will not help.
      </p>

      <h2 class="guide-h2"><Icon name="phone" size={16} />When to call your ISP</h2>
      <p>
        If none of the above work, call your ISP. Tell them your router lights are on but you cannot load any pages. 
        They can check your account, reset your connection from their side, or send a technician if there is a hardware issue.
      </p>
      <p>
        For Jio users, see the <a href="/guides/jio-wifi-not-working/">Jio WiFi not working guide</a> for specific 
        steps and support numbers. For Airtel users, see the <a href="/guides/airtel-wifi-not-working/">Airtel WiFi 
        not working guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="guide-h2"><Icon name="help-circle" size={16} />FAQ</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-q">Why does my WiFi show connected but pages will not load?</h3>
          <p class="faq-a">
            Your device can reach the router, but the router cannot reach the internet. This is usually a stuck connection, 
            a DNS issue, or a problem between the router and the ISP.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-q">How do I know if the problem is my device or the router?</h3>
          <p class="faq-a">
            Check if other devices can load pages. If only one device has the problem, restart that device. 
            If every device shows connected but nothing loads, the router or ISP link is the issue.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-q">What does the LOS light mean on my fiber router?</h3>
          <p class="faq-a">
            LOS stands for Loss of Signal. If the LOS light is on or blinking red, the fiber cable is disconnected 
            or damaged. Check the cable connections and call your ISP if it looks fine.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-q">Will WiFi Hammer make my internet faster?</h3>
          <p class="faq-a">
            No. WiFi Hammer wakes a stuck connection. It does not increase bandwidth or speed up a slow connection.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-related">
        <h3 class="guide-related-title">Related guides</h3>
        <ul class="guide-related-list">
          <li><a href="/guides/jio-wifi-not-working/"><Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} />Jio WiFi not working? Fixes for JioFiber and AirFiber</a></li>
          <li><a href="/guides/airtel-wifi-not-working/"><Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} />Airtel WiFi not working? Fixes for Xstream Fiber</a></li>
          <li><a href="/projects/wifi-hammer/"><Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} />WiFi Hammer Chrome extension</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .guide-page {
    max-width: 48rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .guide-title {
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1.5rem 0;
    color: var(--ink);
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .guide-content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .guide-lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .guide-content p {
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .guide-content a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .guide-content a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-2);
  }

  .guide-h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .faq-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .faq-item {
    padding: 1rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
  }

  .faq-q {
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .faq-a {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .guide-related {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
  }

  .guide-related-title {
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .guide-related-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .guide-related-list a {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .guide-related-list a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .guide-title {
      font-size: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
